23:15:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right. I buried the point. Here’s the plain version, without the consultant-speak. **The blunt truth about scheduling with an Italian team when you’ve been burned before:** - **August doesn’t exist.** Plan for zero output from roughly August 1–20. No amount of incentives or project plans will override Ferragosto. Schedule around it completely. - **Pad every single deadline by 30%, minimum.** Assume their original estimate is optimistic. Don’t ask them to pad it—you pad it on your side when you build the internal plan. - **Never accept a single end date.** Break the work into short, forced-check-in points (every 1–2 weeks) where you see actual, demonstrable progress. No demos, no trust. - **All “yes” is suspicion until you see it.** A polite “perfetto, no problem” often means they didn’t fully understand or haven’t started. Verify with a screenshot, a work-in-progress file, something real. - **Time-zone overlap is tiny—use it brutally.** That one daily window is for decisions, not updates. If something needs clarifying and you don’t get it resolved, assume the day is lost. - **Write down every agreement immediately.** A quick Slack message recapping what you both just said on a call saves you when the “memory” shifts later. - **The biggest slippage risk isn’t laziness—it’s ambiguity.** If the task is even slightly fuzzy, they’ll stop work rather than guess. Be ruthlessly clear about the exact deliverable, format, and test criteria. - **Have a consequence for the first missed mini-deadline.** Not a punishment, but an immediate escalation: a call with your project lead and theirs, a recovery plan written that day. Don’t let small slips slide; they compound. That’s it. No frameworks, no “cadences,” just the lived reality of managing outsourced work in Italy when you need it done on time. If something in this list makes you uncomfortable, that’s the thing that most needs changing in your current setup.
